  <div class="byline">Daniel Abrahamson · StreamStage<br>Why AI? — Dance Teacher Expo 2026<br><b class="mail">daniel@streamstage.live</b></div>
</header>
<div class="thesis">Give yourself back some of your time.</div>

<div class="move"><div class="num">1</div><div>
  <h2>Talk, don't type</h2>
  <p>Put the ChatGPT app on your first screen and always hit the mic — the <b>mic button</b>, not voice mode. Ramble like you're telling a friend; the second you type, it's homework. Same move, bigger: pull your Zoom transcripts, hit record on the teacher meeting.</p>
  <div class="monday">MONDAY: app on first screen · mic, always</div>
</div></div>

<div class="move"><div class="num">2</div><div>
  <h2>Your studio in two files</h2>
  <p>Write a <b>voice file</b> (how you talk, how you sign off, "always dancers, never kids", <b>no em dashes</b>) and a <b>brand file</b> (colours, fonts, the feel). Plain one-pagers <i>you own</i> — drag them into any new chat, forever. Keep both in one folder with your handbook and last year's newsletters.</p>
  <div class="monday">MONDAY: start both files · one page each, boring is fine</div>
</div></div>

<div class="move"><div class="num">3</div><div>
  <h2>Make it write the prompt</h2>
  <p>Don't write the prompt — <b>ask for one</b>. "Here's what I want; write me the prompt that does that, and ask me anything you need first." Then edit <i>its</i> prompt. Same trick writes your two files: "read my website and my last ten newsletters, then write me a brand file and a voice file."</p>
  <div class="monday">MONDAY: next bad answer → fix the <i>prompt</i>, not the answer</div>
</div></div>

<div class="move"><div class="num">4</div><div>
  <h2>Make the poster</h2>
  <p>Nano Banana (inside Gemini) or ChatGPT images — and it now writes the <b>text into the picture</b>. Recital posters, social carousels, schedule graphics. Feed it your brand file and it stops looking like everybody else's AI poster. You don't need to be a designer; you need taste.</p>
  <div class="monday">MONDAY: make the one poster you were going to pay for</div>
</div></div>

<div class="move"><div class="num">5</div><div>
  <h2>Ask your own handbook ★</h2>
  <p>Upload the handbook PDF and ask it the questions your <b>staff</b> ask: "what's the hair for senior jazz on Thursday?" It answers from YOUR document, with the page. Also ask it: "does this contradict itself about refunds?" Notice who <i>can't</i> use this — your parents…</p>
  <div class="monday">MONDAY: upload it · ask the 3 questions your staff ask most</div>
</div></div>

